WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.860
and welcome to your second drink of The Secret.

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.200
You might remember back in the Booze Cruise episode,

3
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.080
Michael finds out that Jim has feelings for Pam.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.780
And this is the episode where Jim is forced

5
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.840
to go to lunch with Michael at Hooters

6
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.460
to make sure that Michael keeps his secret.

7
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.980
Michael is so delighted to have this like bond

8
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.540
with Jim over the secret.

9
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.220
Well, while they're at Hooters, he orders a gourmet hot dog.

10
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.820
And the first time we broke down this episode,

11
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.140
we wondered, does Hooters have gourmet hot dogs?

12
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.760
I couldn't find them on the online website,

13
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.060
but thanks to all of you, we may have solved the mystery.

14
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.540
We got mail.

15
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:42.560
Kenya H.

16
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.720
said, yes, they do,

17
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.480
or at least they did have them and they were delicious.

18
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:48.980
And Liz C.

19
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.480
said, I had a hot dog

20
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.420
at the Coral Gables Hooters in Miami.

21
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.780
So there's two people who have had the Hooters hot dog.

22
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.320
However, Tatiana R.

23
00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:58.720
said,

24
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.880
I worked at Hooters in Las Vegas for several years

25
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.780
and we never served gourmet hot dogs

26
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.340
or ham and cheese sandwiches.

27
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:05.960
Hmm.

28
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.740
It's looking like this is going to be a mom detective's moment.

29
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.800
Well, the Mighty Danny may have solved the mystery.

30
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:12.680
He said,

31
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.500
my wife used to work at Hooters.

32
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.460
She can confirm that they did indeed serve gourmet hot dogs

33
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.400
and ham and cheese sandwiches during that time period.

34
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.780
They were taken off the menu around 2010.

35
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.480
Thank you, the Mighty Danny.

36
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.500
Look at that, a mom detective's moment solved.

37
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.440
Rare, but happens sometimes.

38
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.660
Well, we also talked a lot about casual Fridays

39
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.640
in this breakdown.

40
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.980
Angela, you noted that this episode happens on a Friday

41
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.280
and yet we are not dressed casually.

42
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.780
And a lot of people wrote in to say

43
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.580
that later in a Michael Scott Paper Company episode,

44
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.460
there is a reference to casual Fridays being reinstated.

45
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.300
So somewhere along the line,

46
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.940
it's clear that they were canceled.

47
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.720
Yes, and once we got to Michael Scott Paper Company,

48
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.720
things were explained a little,

49
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.540
but I still stand by my argument

50
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.080
that the whole casual Friday thing

51
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.540
was a little loosey-goosey.

52
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:06.380
I agree.

53
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.660
We also got this letter from Helena N.,

54
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.880
who has this to say about casual Fridays.

55
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.760
Casual Fridays is a hot topic

56
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.200
over in the office ladies' mailbag, let me tell you.

57
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.660
Helena said,

58
00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:18.820
hi, office ladies.

59
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.440
So after listening to the secret episode of your show

60
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.700
three times, I have a theory about casual Fridays.

61
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.100
Helena, thank you for listening three times.

62
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:28.740
I know.

63
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.940
In my mind, casual Fridays are a monthly thing,

64
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.540
but are also an incentive for the office,

65
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.120
like as a reward for a good month's work.

66
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:38.620
Oh.

67
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.920
But because Michael is always getting in trouble

68
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.840
with corporate or having his corporate card taken away,

69
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.440
the whole office is constantly losing this reward.

70
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.100
Do you like this explanation, Angela?

71
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.740
I do, actually.

72
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.140
It makes sense to me that it would be an incentive.

73
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.160
Like, if you reach a certain goal,

74
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.740
everyone is casual Friday.

75
00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.400
That totally makes sense to me.

76
00:02:59.680 --> 00:02:59.920
Yeah.

77
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.860
And I love that she's imagined

78
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.900
that Michael is constantly losing casual Fridays for us.

79
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:06.440
For everyone.

80
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:06.920
Yes.

81
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.760
Although I don't think Angela Martin would mind.

82
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.400
But I think that actually really would have been a funny runner.

83
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:13.460
Yeah, I do too.

84
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.460
Well, you know, lady, there was also a Michael talking head

85
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.840
in the shooting draft where he shares a stat

86
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.620
that he looked up about people missing work on Fridays.

87
00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:23.960
We didn't share it in our first breakdown.

88
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.460
It's such a Michael classic.

89
00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.820
I thought I'd read it to you all.

90
00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.840
So they've just found out that Oscar is missing work

91
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.080
because he's not feeling well,

92
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.620
and it just happens to be a Friday.

93
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.340
So Michael says,

94
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.340
sick on a Friday, starting the weekend early.

95
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.480
Homie, don't play that.

96
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.600
This is a huge problem in offices everywhere.

97
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.240
I looked this up.

98
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.760
Two out of every five sick days

99
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.660
are taken on a Friday or a Monday.

100
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.500
Think about that.

101
00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.500
I bet he's not wrong.

102
00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.280
I bet that's a real stat.

103
00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.480
Okay, everyone, that's all we got.

104
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.420
Please enjoy your second drink of The Secret.

105
00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:05.650
I'm Jenna Fisher.

106
00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:06.970
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

107
00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:08.250
We were on The Office together.

108
00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:09.490
And we're best friends.

109
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.510
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast

110
00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:13.310
just for you.

111
00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:15.810
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office

112
00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.030
and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories

113
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.310
that only two people who were there can tell you.

114
00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:21.450
We're The Office ladies.

115
00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:26.530
Hi, friend.

116
00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:27.810
Good morning, friend.

117
00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:31.190
Today, we're talking about The Secret.

118
00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.210
We kind of have a big day today, you guys.

119
00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.130
We have some fun stuff.

120
00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.090
We have family coming in town, both of us.

121
00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.250
So I think we're feeling all of that.

122
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.610
But we're excited to talk to you about The Secret.

123
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.010
Season 2, Episode 13,

124
00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.110
written by Lee Eisenberg and Gene Stepnitsky,

125
00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.430
directed by Denny Gordon.

126
00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.390
Let's talk a second about Denny Gordon.

127
00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.230
Denny Gordon is my first fast fact.

128
00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:52.930
Let's do it.

129
00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.330
You hit me with a fast fact,

130
00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.190
and then I'll add a little color to it.

131
00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.310
Fast fact number one, directed by Denny Gordon.

132
00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:01.150
Denny is a woman.

133
00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:01.610
Mm-hmm.

134
00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.290
D-E-N-N-I-E, Gordon.

135
00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.870
She would go on to direct Boys and Girls.

136
00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.250
She's also directed several episodes

137
00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:12.550
of John Krasinski's show, Jack Ryan, for Amazon.

138
00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:13.850
She does a lot of dramas.

139
00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:14.250
Yeah.

140
00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.690
Denny was fantastic.

141
00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.030
She was just a wonderful, delightful person to be around.

142
00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.590
I have a great photo of all of us.

143
00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.950
All of us gals gathered around her.

144
00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.750
We were excited to have another female director.

145
00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:28.110
Yep.

146
00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.110
We gathered around her,

147
00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.310
and we have a great picture with her.

148
00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:31.350
I often did this, though.

149
00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.650
It wasn't gender-specific, you guys.

150
00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.530
I have photos with us with all the directors.

151
00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:36.830
Yes, you.

152
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.230
I do.

153
00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.390
You would take photos with the male directors as well.

154
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:39.730
Yeah.

155
00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:41.050
You did not discriminate.

156
00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.010
I did not discriminate.

157
00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.170
I have a great picture of me with Harold Ramis,

158
00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.690
but Denny was just such a delight,

159
00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:46.830
and I wanted a photo with her.

160
00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:50.670
We actually had only six female directors

161
00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.790
through the nine years of The Office,

162
00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.950
and they were Mindy Kaling,

163
00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:57.510
Jen Salata,

164
00:05:57.950 --> 00:05:58.730
Amy Heckerling,

165
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:00.130
Claire Scanlon,

166
00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:01.770
Kelly Cantley,

167
00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:03.570
and Denny Gordon.

168
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:06.490
So, ladies out there directing,

169
00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.530
we love you.

170
00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:08.010
We love you.

171
00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:08.630
Keep doing it.

172
00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:09.210
Yeah.

173
00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:10.530
Let's get some more ladies directing.

174
00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.810
That's my little speech there.

175
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:13.790
I love it.

176
00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.130
Denny was and is a trailblazer

177
00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.410
in terms of female directors.

178
00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.010
She's been around a really long time,

179
00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.970
and when researching this episode,

180
00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.390
I ended up reading a bunch of articles

181
00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:27.650
and interviews with her

182
00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.230
about her experience.

183
00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:31.010
Her journey.

184
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:31.810
Yeah, I did too.

185
00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.530
And it was very inspiring.

186
00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.310
So, guys, Google her.

187
00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:35.990
Yeah.

188
00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:37.830
D-E-N-N-I-E, Gordon.

189
00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.010
Denny, if you hear this,

190
00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:41.770
we're sending you some love.

191
00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:42.610
All right.

192
00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:43.630
Where are we at, Jenna?

193
00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.030
Fast fact number two.

194
00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.870
We've got a big guest star.

195
00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:47.850
We're going to introduce

196
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.210
a new recurring character,

197
00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:50.550
Tom Chick,

198
00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:51.770
who plays Gil.

199
00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:53.130
Yes.

200
00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.370
Oscar's boyfriend.

201
00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:56.850
Oscar's love intereSt.

202
00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:58.090
It's a secret.

203
00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.310
He appeared in four episodes

204
00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:01.790
as Oscar's boyfriend, Gil.

205
00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.830
He also had a recurring role

206
00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.570
as a reporter on The West Wing.

207
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.750
So you might recognize him from that.

208
00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:09.690
But here's the crazy thing about him.

209
00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.450
His big passion in life

210
00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.990
is video gaming.

211
00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:15.050
Okay.

212
00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:16.970
And he is a very famous

213
00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.570
video gaming writer.

214
00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:20.330
He's written tons of articles,

215
00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:21.950
tons of blogs.

216
00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.630
He's like a video gaming critic.

217
00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:26.570
This is amazing.

218
00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:27.270
Fascinating.

219
00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.970
This just proves, guys,

220
00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:30.370
I always say this,

221
00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:31.670
you don't know me.

222
00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.210
You don't know someone.

223
00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.170
You have no idea

224
00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.870
how layered and complex people are.

225
00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:36.970
I had no idea.

226
00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:37.370
Yeah.

227
00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:37.890
Wow.

228
00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:38.870
That's cool.

229
00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:39.490
All right.

230
00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:40.930
Are we ready for fast fact number three?

231
00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:41.370
Yes.

232
00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.310
Steve Carell was nominated

233
00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:45.010
for a Golden Globe

234
00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:46.450
for playing Michael Scott.

235
00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:47.810
This was his first nomination

236
00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.090
for playing Michael Scott

237
00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.890
the week we shot this episode.

238
00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:54.670
And then he won the Golden Globe

239
00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.730
the week this episode aired.

240
00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:57.530
Yes.

241
00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.250
This was a huge upset shocker win.

242
00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:01.930
First of all,

243
00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:03.110
the fact that he was nominated

244
00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.290
was insane

245
00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:05.390
because he was nominated

246
00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:07.890
for those six episodes

247
00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:09.070
from season one.

248
00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:09.790
You have to remember,

249
00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:11.110
we were barely on the air.

250
00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:12.310
We were going to get canceled

251
00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:13.110
at any minute

252
00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:14.490
and we didn't have

253
00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.270
a lot of clout around town.

254
00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:16.790
No.

255
00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:17.690
Not at all.

256
00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:19.390
So this was like an upset

257
00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:20.390
and we were thrilled.

258
00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:21.010
It couldn't happen

259
00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:21.950
to a nicer person.

260
00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:23.790
Steve's acceptance speech

261
00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.450
is amazing.

262
00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.010
You can go on YouTube

263
00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.050
and type in

264
00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:28.810
Steve Carell's

265
00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:29.470
Golden Globes

266
00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:30.230
acceptance speech

267
00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:31.170
for 2006.

268
00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:32.670
He gets up there

269
00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:33.570
and he's like,

270
00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.650
I didn't really know

271
00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:34.990
